WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.800
[Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.850
TAVI: I can't sing.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.750
MERC: Lots of people say that.

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:13.127
Asked, most of 1,105 students
said they couldn't copy a tune.

00:00:13.627 --> 00:00:16.597
TAVI: So what happened
when researchers tested it?

00:00:16.897 --> 00:00:17.966
MERC: Let's find out.

00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:21.566
[chime]

00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:25.113
By the end,
you'll know how your voice makes a note,

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:29.313
how to look after it
and what 'in tune' means.

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:32.478
And you can sing 3 notes back to us.

00:00:33.578 --> 00:00:37.265
First, a question from last time.
Listen to two steps.

00:00:37.348 --> 00:00:39.108
Which one is the semitone?

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.858
[two steps]

00:00:43.058 --> 00:00:44.858
[think]

00:00:45.158 --> 00:00:52.459
The second. B to C: two white keys,
but only one semitone, 100 cents.

00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:59.334
The amber band is my note. Listen:
my A, then a reply on the same note.

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:01.334
[Merc sings an A]

00:01:01.534 --> 00:01:03.134
[a reply on the same A]

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:05.952
And a reply a semitone below.

00:01:06.352 --> 00:01:07.952
[Merc sings an A]

00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:09.752
[a reply a semitone below]

00:01:10.152 --> 00:01:13.706
That's the study's cut-off:
a semitone or more off.

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:16.463
In one test, 39 students

00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:20.432
who said they couldn't copy a note
sang short tunes back.

00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:28.169
Predict before you see the result.
How many sang a semitone or more off?

00:01:28.252 --> 00:01:31.915
Most of them,
or about as few as anyone else?

00:01:32.215 --> 00:01:34.215
[pause and predict]

00:01:34.615 --> 00:01:35.797
Only 4 of them.

00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:40.904
Of 40 others,
not picked for saying so, 6 did.

00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:43.411
Saying so didn't predict how they sang.

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:47.988
In the tests, only about
one in ten to one in six

00:01:48.071 --> 00:01:50.473
missed by a semitone or more.

00:01:50.973 --> 00:01:54.041
TAVI: Could the ones
who sang off hear the difference?

00:01:54.441 --> 00:01:56.138
MERC: Most of them could.

00:01:56.221 --> 00:02:00.131
They heard small changes in pitch
as well as the accurate singers did.

00:02:00.431 --> 00:02:04.818
The hard part was turning a heard note
into a sung one:

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:07.905
the link between ear and voice.

00:02:08.405 --> 00:02:14.446
Congenital amusia, the hearing condition
the term 'tone deaf' properly names,

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.916
affects about 1.5 % of people.

00:02:19.816 --> 00:02:22.212
TAVI: Where does my voice's note
come from?

00:02:22.612 --> 00:02:25.569
MERC: Every note starts
with something shaking.

00:02:25.652 --> 00:02:28.015
In your voice, it's your vocal folds.

00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:32.592
When you breathe, they're apart.
When you sing, they come together,

00:02:32.675 --> 00:02:35.685
and air from your lungs
sets them vibrating.

00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:38.085
[Merc sings an A]

00:02:38.485 --> 00:02:45.090
For the A, 220 Hz,
they open and close 220 times a second.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.640
Slowed 220 times, that's once a second.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.940
[a tick, once a second]

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.303
TAVI: So how does a voice go higher?

00:02:54.703 --> 00:02:57.810
MERC: Muscles stretch
the folds longer and tighter,

00:02:57.893 --> 00:03:02.358
and tighter folds vibrate faster,
like a guitar string tuned up.

00:03:02.858 --> 00:03:06.627
TAVI: Wait,
doesn't a longer string sound lower?

00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:08.704
MERC: On its own, yes.

00:03:08.787 --> 00:03:13.397
But stretching also tightens the folds,
and the tightness counts for more.

00:03:13.797 --> 00:03:16.397
[Merc's note slides up an octave]

00:03:16.797 --> 00:03:21.995
At 440 Hz, slowed the same way:
twice a second.

00:03:22.295 --> 00:03:24.295
[a tick, twice a second]

00:03:24.795 --> 00:03:27.942
But when you sing,
don't think about your folds.

00:03:28.025 --> 00:03:29.892
Aim for the note you hear.

00:03:30.492 --> 00:03:32.192
TAVI: Do I need to sing loudly?

00:03:32.592 --> 00:03:35.769
MERC: No. Loud and high
are different things.

00:03:35.852 --> 00:03:37.671
Listen: louder.

00:03:38.021 --> 00:03:39.621
[Merc's A gets louder]

00:03:39.921 --> 00:03:41.050
Then higher.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.900
[Merc's note steps higher]

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.771
A quiet note is just as much
the right note as a loud one.

00:03:48.271 --> 00:03:52.848
So when you sing along,
sing at about the volume you talk at:

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:55.320
no shouting, no whispering.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.937
Keep water nearby, and sip it.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:03.496
Rest when your voice feels tired,
and stop if it feels scratchy or hoarse.

00:04:03.996 --> 00:04:08.566
Sore, hoarse or unwell today?
Just listen along.

00:04:11.466 --> 00:04:15.075
TAVI: If my ears are fine,
why is my note off?

00:04:15.475 --> 00:04:19.526
MERC: Because hearing a note
and making it are different jobs.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:23.413
Matching is a loop.
Hear my note, and try it.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:29.462
Compare: higher, lower or the same?
Then adjust, and compare again.

00:04:29.962 --> 00:04:32.229
Watch a line chase my note.

00:04:32.629 --> 00:04:34.046
[Merc sings an A]

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:38.129
[a line slides up to Merc's A]

00:04:38.529 --> 00:04:47.283
80 cents low. Compare, and adjust:
40 low. Adjust again: 12 low, in the band.

00:04:47.783 --> 00:04:50.460
Singing in tune is a skill:

00:04:50.543 --> 00:04:54.180
the link between hearing a note
and making it.

00:04:54.263 --> 00:04:56.894
And it can get better with practice.

00:04:57.394 --> 00:05:02.284
TAVI: So it's not my ears. It's the link,
and the link can be practised?

00:05:02.684 --> 00:05:04.411
MERC: For most people, yes.

00:05:07.311 --> 00:05:10.549
TAVI: An octave up is 1,200 cents
from your note.

00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:12.332
Does that still count?

00:05:12.732 --> 00:05:18.014
MERC: Predict before you listen.
Does it count, or is it too far off?

00:05:18.314 --> 00:05:20.314
[pause and predict]

00:05:20.714 --> 00:05:22.614
[Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

00:05:22.964 --> 00:05:24.864
[Tavi sings an A, 440 Hz]

00:05:25.364 --> 00:05:27.264
[Merc and Tavi sing together]

00:05:27.664 --> 00:05:33.851
It counts: the same letter,
twice as fast, one turn up the spiral.

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.991
It does sound higher:
that's what makes it tricky.

00:05:37.074 --> 00:05:39.875
Voice Mirror folds it into my octave.

00:05:40.375 --> 00:05:44.702
Two people singing one tune
are often an octave apart,

00:05:44.785 --> 00:05:48.182
and the researchers
counted octave matches too.

00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:51.039
When we sing at the end, pick the A,

00:05:51.122 --> 00:05:53.754
the one that feels easier
to hum along with.

00:05:56.654 --> 00:05:59.061
TAVI: How close is close enough?

00:05:59.144 --> 00:06:01.931
Do accurate singers
land right on the line?

00:06:02.331 --> 00:06:05.748
MERC: Even the accurate singers
in the study's second test

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:09.282
sat about 30 cents from the target,
on average.

00:06:09.582 --> 00:06:15.592
So 'in tune' can't mean one exact line.
It's a band around the note.

00:06:16.092 --> 00:06:22.119
In this lesson, as in Voice Mirror,
the band reaches 35 cents either side:

00:06:22.202 --> 00:06:24.305
about a third of a semitone.

00:06:24.705 --> 00:06:28.355
The study's cut-off,
a semitone, is 100 cents:

00:06:28.438 --> 00:06:31.765
nearly 3 times as far as the band's edge.

00:06:34.665 --> 00:06:37.504
Your turn: 3 notes, slowly.

00:06:38.004 --> 00:06:42.228
Sore, hoarse or unwell today?
Just listen along.

00:06:42.728 --> 00:06:45.445
Slow helps. In one study,

00:06:45.528 --> 00:06:48.675
most occasional singers
sang a well-known song

00:06:48.758 --> 00:06:52.492
as accurately as professionals
once they slowed down.

00:06:52.992 --> 00:06:55.669
Sing at about the volume you talk at,

00:06:55.752 --> 00:06:59.482
and stop immediately
if anything feels uncomfortable.

00:06:59.982 --> 00:07:04.349
Listen to each note,
then sing it back for about three seconds,

00:07:04.432 --> 00:07:09.566
with whichever of us feels easier.
Nothing listens.

00:07:10.166 --> 00:07:11.557
First, A.

00:07:11.957 --> 00:07:13.957
[Merc sings an A]

00:07:14.307 --> 00:07:16.307
[Tavi sings the A an octave up]

00:07:16.707 --> 00:07:21.207
[your turn: sing the A]

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:24.117
Now G, a tone below.

00:07:24.517 --> 00:07:26.517
[Merc sings a G]

00:07:26.867 --> 00:07:28.867
[Tavi sings the G an octave up]

00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:33.767
[your turn: sing the G]

00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:36.925
And B, a tone above the A.

00:07:37.325 --> 00:07:39.325
[Merc sings a B]

00:07:39.675 --> 00:07:41.675
[Tavi sings the B an octave up]

00:07:42.075 --> 00:07:46.575
[your turn: sing the B]

00:07:47.175 --> 00:07:51.642
If any of that felt uncomfortable,
that's enough singing for today:

00:07:51.725 --> 00:07:53.168
rest your voice.

00:08:03.768 --> 00:08:04.638
Quick check.

00:08:05.138 --> 00:08:09.435
TAVI: When I sing a higher note,
what are my vocal folds doing?

00:08:09.735 --> 00:08:11.535
[think]

00:08:11.835 --> 00:08:15.285
MERC: Vibrating faster:
stretched longer and tighter.

00:08:15.885 --> 00:08:20.090
Someone says,
'I can't sing.' What's most likely?

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:22.190
[think]

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:26.861
Their ears are probably fine.
The link to the voice needs practice.

00:08:27.461 --> 00:08:34.068
I sing an A3. You sing an A2,
an octave lower and 20 cents high.

00:08:34.151 --> 00:08:35.384
Where is your note?

00:08:35.684 --> 00:08:38.184
[think]

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:43.454
In the band. An octave away counts,
and 20 cents is inside it.

00:08:43.954 --> 00:08:47.821
When your voice feels comfortable,
practise in Voice Mirror:

00:08:47.904 --> 00:08:53.175
it has you glide and hold a note,
then plays 5 notes to sing back.

00:08:53.575 --> 00:08:57.552
Keep the glides comfortable.
10 minutes, gently, most days.

00:08:57.635 --> 00:08:59.722
Missed a day? Just come back.

00:09:00.722 --> 00:09:01.652
Next time.

00:09:02.052 --> 00:09:06.100
I climb seven stairs and stop,
one short of home.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:10.400
[Merc climbs seven notes and stops]

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:15.557
TAVI: Why does do-re-mi sound finished
when it lands on do,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.483
and restless when it stops on ti?

00:09:18.883 --> 00:09:21.800
[chime]
